These patches apply to bryangerlach/rdgen (https://github.com/bryangerlach/rdgen, GPL-3.0) and are GPL-3.0 like the
rest of this repository. UPSTREAM names the commit they apply to. The host script copies this directory into the CT,
where rdgen-setup applies them.

The base series, 0001-0013, is the hardening and the pull model:
0001 secrets come from a root-only env file, and insecure defaults are refused
0002 login everywhere, CSRF back on, a token or a session for /api
0003 strict server-side rules for every value that reaches a build script
0004 the pull model: the encrypted config goes to GitHub as a workflow input
0005 a poller downloads the build artifacts and then deletes them; status and downloads come from the database
0006 the push model's inbound callback endpoints are removed
0007 the workflows take the config from that input, and the decrypt step checks it again
0008 tools/pullify.py, which turns upstream's workflows into pull-model ones (see below)
0009 pullify's output, generated, with the trailer "Rdgen-Mechanical: pullify": on a newer upstream it is regenerated,
     not rebased
0010 a native runtime for Alpine 3.24 (OpenRC) and Debian 13 (systemd), no Docker
0011 tools/rdgen-github-bootstrap, which sets up the private build repo and applies upstream updates
0012 the test suite, with a local GitHub API stub
0013 SETUP-PULL.md: the GitHub setup, the two tokens, the native install, updates

Besides the pull model and actions pinned to commits, pullify:
- stages custom_.txt next to the renamed Linux program (0016), and on macOS in Contents/Resources, where RustDesk reads
  it. Upstream used Contents/MacOS, which code signing treats as code, so pullify also drops upstream's rcodesign
  "Sign macOS app bundle" step and signs with codesign instead.
- keeps the Linux DRM job (an extra unattended-wayland .deb, RustDesk 1.5.0 and later) from failing builds. It puts
  back upstream's "Detect DRM support" step when nothing sets DRM_SUPPORTED, builds only where RustDesk has --drm,
  skips the job in ARM-only builds and marks it continue-on-error. On a Linux runner of your own it runs only with
  the repository variable RDGEN_LINUX_DRM=true.
- applies Flutter's dropdown patch only where the SDK doesn't have it yet, since a self-hosted runner keeps the SDK
  from its last build.
- sets rust-cache's cache-bin to false on self-hosted runners, where its cleanup deleted rustup after a successful job.
- turns every download of a patch file from upstream master into a copy from the build repository.

The later patches, newest last:
0014 hands finished builds to CortenDesk's Client Downloads (`manage.py rdgen_publish`, used by rdgen-publish).
0015 fills in the port, link, update URL and company fields from RDGEN_DEFAULT_*, explains every server field, adds the
     settings reference and the as-you-type checks to the Other section, and fixes "Deny LAN discovery" and
     "Automatically close incoming sessions", which did nothing upstream.
0016 gives Linux builds a Linux-safe app name (the packaging and the client's runtime both need one) and refuses names
     that would replace a system command.
0017 adds the Recent builds page (/builds) and expiring share links (/share/<token>, no login). The console passes
     only those two share paths to rdgen (RDGEN_SHARE_BASE, /etc/nginx/rustdesk-clients.conf).
0018 lets Recent builds delete a finished or failed build: its files and share links at once, the row at the usual
     retention.
0019 explains the API server (with a warning for http:// host names), the connection type, where the permanent
     password belongs (with a live note when it is unused) and what a build costs in Actions minutes.
0020 adds the form's "Mac builds" choice (input mac_arch). pullify builds the Mac matrix from it, lets repository
     variables send Mac jobs to a self-hosted runner, and on a self-hosted Mac checks for Xcode and the tools
     tools/runner-mac.sh installs instead of running brew install.
0021 lets Linux and Windows jobs (and the helper jobs) run on self-hosted runners through repository variables, adds
     the Linux build choices (linux_arch, linux_formats), and skips GitHub-image-only steps there.
0022 records which runner ran each job of a build, so the form's cost note says where the last build of the chosen
     kind ran instead of always quoting GitHub's prices.
0023 refuses Windows app names that RustDesk 1.5.0 can't install (anything but letters, digits and hyphens), where
     "Accept and install" would silently do nothing.
0024 lets upstream's self-hosted update key (update_public_key, /api/generate only) through both checks: an ed25519
     public key in base64, else empty. Without the runner rule every build stopped at "Load Secrets" ("unknown keys").
     With a key, upstream's allow_custom_update.py points the client's update check at
     <downloadLink>/api/version/latest (a rustdesk-api endpoint; CortenDesk has none) and trusts metadata signed with
     that key.
0025 runs the test suite in the bootstrap's verify step (init and the daily update), so an upstream change that
     applies cleanly but breaks builds keeps the current version instead of reaching the CT.
0026 has the setup job refuse an app_version that is not x.y.z or x.y.z.w (a dispatch input outside the encrypted
     config, pasted into sed by upstream's "Set custom app version" step), and makes ghstub accept only the inputs
     the workflows declare.
0027 gives upstream's build-config key slug_appname (the app name as a slug, pasted into the workflows' sed commands) a
     rule in both validation.py and validate.py, computed from the name the build really gets.
0028 adjusts the tests to upstream's 1.5.0-only form. Windows names with spaces are refused on 1.5.0, so the tests that
     need such a name use macOS. It also counts the new key.

tools/rdgen-rebase.py moves the series to a newer upstream: it rebases, regenerates 0009, exports and verifies.
.github/workflows/rdgen-upstream.yml runs it when a CT asks, or by hand. patch-source, installed next to the patches in
the CT with github_known_hosts, lets a CT follow this directory on a GitHub branch: rdgen-setup --patch-source
<owner/repo>.
